# Rank 1691 / CVE-2026-26267 (GHSA-4chv-4c6w-w254, recorded CWE-670; the
# measured shape is wrong-function resolution at an exported entry point):
# soroban-sdk's #[contractimpl] proc macro emits the WASM export wrapper for a
# trait impl block as `<Type>::func()`, and Rust's name resolution prefers an
# inherent associated function over a trait function for that syntax, so a
# contract whose type carries an inherent method sharing the trait method's
# name exports the INHERENT implementation: the on-chain entry point runs
# different code than the #[contractimpl] block annotated. e92a3933 ("Fix
# trait method call resolution in contractimpl (#1729)", 2026-02-14) emits
# `<Type as Trait>::func()` when a trait is in play. The vulnerable construct
# is present at the parent (soroban-sdk-macros/src/derive_fn.rs:52, `let call
# = quote! { <#impl_ty>::#ident };`) and the remediation at the commit.
#
# ATTENTION, rechecked: no layer can see the defect, three ways measured
# afresh at both revisions of the real checkout.
#
# 1. Where the change lands (the proc macro), the only discriminator between
#    the revisions is the fixing commit's own layout. The unqualified token
#    text `quote! { <#impl_ty>::#ident }` is still present at the commit --
#    it is the else branch of the same `let` -- so any token- or text-keyed
#    binding fires identically on both revisions; only the presence of
#    `as #t` (or the `if let Some(t) = trait_ident` wrapper, or the removal
#    of the `use_trait` let) distinguishes, and each of those keys on the
#    fix's own spelling of a private metavariable. Measured over three
#    --cache off runs per revision: 1398 findings / 1491 flags / 143 at or
#    above high on the parent and the identical triple on the fixed revision,
#    deterministic run to run; the only differences between the revisions are
#    line shifts in tests-expanded/ (which the commit regenerates) and the
#    two VYQL-MEM-006 flags in derive_fn.rs moving :58/:116 to :62/:120. The
#    only findings inside soroban-sdk-macros are two VYQL-PATH-001
#    FilePathAccess rows at lib.rs:559/:673, identical at both revisions and
#    about a build-time file read, not the resolution defect.
#
# 2. Where the defect manifests (consumer contracts), the trigger is an
#    inherent method colliding with a #[contractimpl] trait-impl method on
#    the same type. An exhaustive sweep of every impl in the tree at the
#    parent (270 .rs files, 13 #[contractimpl] trait impls) finds zero
#    same-file collisions. The tree-wide name-only join produces seven
#    cross-file hits, every one joining DISTINCT per-module `Contract` types
#    (contract_duration.rs, contract_timepoint.rs, the _migrating.rs `//!`
#    doc comment, tests/auth, tests-expanded/test_auth_tests.rs) -- the
#    name-join trap, not a collision. The same sweep at the fixed revision
#    (271 files, 14 impls) finds exactly one same-file collision: the
#    regression test the commit itself adds
#    (soroban-sdk/src/tests/contractimpl_trait_call_resolution.rs, `value`).
#    A consumer-side binding therefore reports MORE on the fixed revision:
#    inverted discrimination, and an empty reachable set at the vulnerable
#    revision outside this incident.
#
# 3. The facts a consumer-side binding would need do not exist, re-verified
#    against the current definitions tip: the same attribute on a function
#    yields an analysis.parameter.entry fact a binding can key on
#    (args.any.context.attrName, the generated httpInput* idiom), while the
#    identical attribute on an impl block yields zero facts
#    (vyql query -call analysis.parameter.entry: 1 node with `#[attr] fn`,
#    0 with `#[attr] impl`). stmtH's "impl_item" case
#    (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/rust.go) still ignores the attrs
#    its caller collected and re-walks the body with a fresh decls(), and no
#    fact carries the impl's trait or self type. That is the Rust
#    no-impl-level-fact family recorded at rank 1364, whose entry already
#    names this rank, so no new entry. Even with impl attributes and impl
#    identity, the collision itself is a cross-item same-name join between
#    two sibling impl blocks that only real type identity can validate --
#    the sweep's seven false hits are what a name-only join produces -- and
#    no binding construct expresses it (scopeCall is call-shaped and
#    same-or-nested lexical scopes).
#
# The blocks below pin the measured zeros on the real shapes. The rejects name
# VYQL-MEM-006, the rule family that reports most in this repository (992 of
# the 1398 findings at the parent), to assert the scanner reads these files
# and judges nothing about the defect. The first block is the fixed
# revision's own regression contract (the trigger shape); when an
# impl-identity fact and a collision/export rule exist, it must flip to
# expect. The second block is the repository's real no-collision trait impl
# (CustomAccountInterface). The third is the safe inherent-impl form that any
# naive contractimpl-keyed binding would have to stay silent on -- telling it
# apart from the trait form needs exactly the impl trait-identity fact that
# is missing.
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1691: a contractimpl trait method sharing a name with an inherent method"
  lang rust
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  use soroban_sdk::{contract, contractimpl, Env};

  #[contract]
  pub struct Contract;

  impl Contract {
      pub fn value(_env: Env) -> u32 {
          1
      }
  }

  pub trait ContractTrait {
      fn value(env: Env) -> u32;
  }

  #[contractimpl]
  impl ContractTrait for Contract {
      fn value(_env: Env) -> u32 {
          2
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1691: a contractimpl trait method with no same-named inherent method"
  lang rust
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  use soroban_sdk::{
      auth::{Context, CustomAccountInterface},
      contract, contracterror, contractimpl,
      crypto::Hash,
      Env, Vec,
  };

  #[contract]
  pub struct Contract;

  #[contracterror]
  #[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord)]
  pub enum Error {
      Fail = 1,
  }

  #[contractimpl]
  impl CustomAccountInterface for Contract {
      type Signature = ();
      type Error = Error;

      fn __check_auth(
          _env: Env,
          _signature_payload: Hash<32>,
          _signatures: Self::Signature,
          _auth_contexts: Vec<Context>,
      ) -> Result<(), Self::Error> {
          Ok(())
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1691: a contractimpl inherent impl, where unqualified resolution is correct"
  lang rust
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  use soroban_sdk::{contract, contractimpl, Duration};

  #[contract]
  pub struct Contract;

  #[contractimpl]
  impl Contract {
      pub fn exec(_d: Duration) {}
  }
  ```
